Consider:

- whether the feature needs to exist and whether existing behavior already covers it;
- viable implementation approaches and meaningful tradeoffs;
- compatibility, migration, and breaking-change consequences;
- prerequisite refactoring that would let the feature fit naturally;
- reuse of stdlib, platform capabilities, and installed dependencies;
- whether an available third-party dependency should be proposed to the user;
- security, state corruption, data loss, reliability, stability, performance, and user-error risks;
- alternatives that should be rejected as unnecessary or over-engineered.

Recommend one approach when the evidence supports it. Identify every decision that needs human judgment. Produce a complete solution analysis; do not merely describe changes from the prior analysis.
